but  how often will they happen in the future  well covid-19 become seasonal like flu  that seems likely though we don't know  how long exactly that process will take  Health authorities do mostly agree that  otherwise healthy people who recover  from covid-19 are generally at low risk  of getting it again for at least a few  months but each person is going to have  a different immunological history with a  disease so it's impossible to really  predict your individual risk and if  immune evasion turns out to happen in  different ways with different  sub-variants experts say you can't  really rule out possibly getting  infected by them even in Fairly short  order Omicron at its sub-variants though  have really kind of thrown a monkey  wrench into what we thought we knew  because they're super infectious and  several look like they're better at  evading immunity than earlier versions  of the virus and and Omicron also  continues to mutate at a pretty fast  pace that ability the experts say could  easily cause numbers to to spike pretty  hard and fast in the coming winter in  the northern hemisphere  when it comes to disease severity upon  reinfection however the news is more  positive most studies I've read show  that when reinfection does happen the  course of the disease in general seems  to be milder  [Music]  residents of Islam in the Venezuelan  Capital Caracas found a new way to stay  in contact despite pandemic restrictions  they came up with the idea of starting a  Rooftop Cinema  the project turned out to be so popular  that it's still going strong  the red brick Shanty homes rise steeply  on the hills of patare in the east of  Caracas  home to Jimmy Perez patari is one of the  largest slums in the world the flat  rooftops here are called Plata bandas  these Open Spaces gained new  significance during the covid-19  pandemic  it's a place on the roof of a house made  with construction materials  in the populated areas the platavanda is  a social space where people celebrate  birthdays 15 years birthdays carnival  activities take place here  the platform the Halls people's memories  it guards a family's most treasured  feelings  the rooftops became the base for the  project cine Plata Banda launched in the  first months of the pandemic it used  Outdoor Cinema to relieve residents  confined to their homes  the events have continued beyond the  quarantines and lockdowns and expanded  to include live music more than 200  screenings have now been held with  several scheduled each month  the project was a reaction to the  increased level of violence and suicide  experienced during the pandemic the  Project's co-organizer and Jimmy's twin  brother Jaime explains the program  there are children films and documentary  films one important film which deals  with the fight against oppression  this is for a population that is living  through trauma and going through the  process of mourning and sadness  also the scars left by Venezuelan social  situation  all of that was projected into our Plata  Banda cinema screen which was welcomed  by the community had generated awareness  what's the level of violence was reduced  as the film ends residents flashed their  lights to applaud and not only for the  entertainment they are grateful for the  connections the rooftop events have  created  sometimes there may be events which I  can't see from my house  we speak with our neighbors who are  watching  and we move to their house and sit  together because they have a big balcony  with a good view  we already knew each other but now we  share more  this rooftop project allows us to share  more have more contact  and now we're more integrated  the pandemic intensified the social  problems which have plagued potato gangs  used the lockdown to step up criminal  activity  pitare known as Venezuela's the most  dangerous place became even more  insecure through Sydney plattabanda the  community reclaimed public space the  organizers say their initiative showed  patari's residence how to take action  where Public Services fail  I aspired to see violence reduced  is the only way if they're adding People  Like Us who take care of important  topics necessary topics then we will be  in appetite without hope and without  future  we aspire to have a more Humane  Community with a better education with a  better opportunity where people feel  useful  and we are showing that without  government without being part of  structures we have demonstrated great  achievement significant and important  achievement for the people for the  community for children for caretakers  and for parents  the cineplata banda team Drive their  equipment to one of the poorest parts of  the giant settlement  where their excited audience awaits  this screening is for the children who  hardly get to visit a real Cinema  donors have signed up to provide food  for kids who frequently go hungry  many parents are without work they can't  take care of their families  there are children who go to bed without  eating  when I started coming here they told me  Glenda I've gone two days without eating  two days without food it's something he  has experienced  they give us a lot and now we're bigger  and stronger  this Cinema project is shining a light  on how a community can come together  even in the darkest moments  that's it for today see you next week on  the covid-19 special when we'll show you  other ways in which people across the  world supported each other during the  pandemic  in a Buenos Aires soup kitchen for  example  thanks for watching and stay healthy  [Music]

